  1625\n   ```\n\nSo, using the long multiplication method, the product of 25 and 65 is 1625.\n\n</think>.\n<answer>. Therefore, using the long multiplication method, the product of 25 and 65 is 1625.</answer>.<|User|>Thank you for the detailed explanation and examples. Can you also provide a brief explanation of why the distributive property works in this context?<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c><think>.\nCertainly! The distributive property of multiplication over addition states that for any three numbers \\(a\\), \\(b\\), and \\(c\\), the equation \\(a(b + c) = ab + ac\\) holds true. This property allows us to break down a multiplication problem into simpler parts, making it easier to compute.\n\nIn the context of solving \\(25 \\times 65\\), we can use the distributive property to express 65 as \\(60 + 5\\). This gives us:\n\\[ 25 \\times 65 = 25 \\times (60 + 5) \\]\n\nApplying the distributive property:\n\\[ 25 \\times (60 + 5) = (25 \\times 60) + (25 \\times 5) \\]\n\nWe already know from the previous methods that:\n\\[ 25 \\times 60 = 1500 \\]\n\\[ 25 \\times 5 = 125 \\]\n\nAdding these results together:\n\\[ 1500 + 125 = 1625 \\]\n\nThis shows that the distributive property allows us to break down the multiplication into simpler multiplications and additions, which can be easier to compute mentally or on paper. This property is fundamental in algebra and simplifies many mathematical operations.\n\n</think>.\n<answer>. The distributive property works in this context because it allows us to break down the multiplication of 25 and 65 into simpler multiplications and additions. By expressing 65 as \\(60 + 5\\), we can use the distributive property to write \\(25 \\times 65\\) as \\(25 \\times (60 + 5) = (25 \\times 60) + (25 \\times 5)\\). This simplifies the calculation and makes it easier to compute the final result, which is 1625.</answer>",
